🛡️ Module 1: Strategic Triage & Statistics
Before looking at individual packets, an analyst must understand the "Top Talkers" on a network
. TShark’s statistics module allows you to map entire conversations in seconds.
Command: TCP Conversation Mapping
tshark -r incident.pcap -q -z conv,tcp
Operational Goal: Generates a summarized list of all TCP conversations
.
Analyst Insight: Sort by Bytes to instantly identify Data Exfiltration events where an internal host is uploading massive volumes to an external IP
.
🧬 Module 2: Surgical Field Extraction
TShark’s power lies in its ability to ignore the noise and extract only the specific metadata required for a lead.
Scenario 1: Hunting DNS Tunneling (C2 Detection)
Attackers use long, random subdomains to smuggle data through the DNS protocol
.
tshark -r capture.pcap -Y "dns.flags.response == 0" -T fields -e frame.time -e ip.src -e dns.qry.name > dns_audit.txt
Code Breakdown:
-Y: Applies a display filter for DNS queries
.
-T fields: Tells TShark to output specific columns
.
-e dns.qry.name: Extracts only the queried domain
.
Operational Goal: Audit domain lookups for labels exceeding 50 characters, a primary indicator of DNS Tunneling
.
Scenario 2: Web Credential Harvesting
Identifying unencrypted POST requests to hunt for stolen credentials
.
tshark -r web_traffic.pcap -Y 'http.request.method == "POST"' -T fields -e ip.src -e ip.dst -e http.host -e http.request.uri
Operational Goal: Monitors all outbound data submissions
. If a device with "no business" uploading data sends large POST requests to a foreign IP, it indicates a theft event
.
🏹 Module 3: Automated Threat Hunting
By chaining TShark with Linux commands like grep, awk, and sort, you can build automated detectors for common attack patterns
.
Detection: Identifying Nmap SYN Scans
tshark -r traffic.pcap -Y "tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0" | awk '{print $3}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr
Analyst Logic: This filter isolates SYN packets without corresponding ACKs
. Using awk and sort reveals which source IP is sending the most "knocks" on digital doors, confirming the Reconnaissance phase of an attack in real-time
.
